    "product_id": "bde0368f-8a5e-4211-8c20-567ad8e6cd15",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 032321\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n421 PM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 00Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Flooding of several area streams and rivers continues to\n  subside, while major waterways like the Salt River and Gila\n  River will likely continue to experience flooding through the \n  week due to dam releases.\n \n- Dry weather with seasonable temperatures will continue the next\n  several days with lower desert highs warming into upper 70s to \n  middle 80s by the end of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \n\nA considerable volume of water continues to flow downhill through\nmany of the major waterways in south-central AZ early this\nafternoon, especially in the Salt and Verde systems. Salt River \nProject releases from the Bartlett Dam into the Verde River and \nGranite Reef Dam into the Salt River, remain very high, over 30000\ncfs and over 55000 cfs respectively. Streamflow gauges were also \nreading between 20000-50000 cfs downstream from Tempe Town Lake \nheading into the Gila River. The last time there were major \nreleases this high was in the Spring of 2023 and the Granite Reef \nDam releases peaked at just under 40000 cfs and the runoff lasted \nfor 2-3 months before fully emptying into the Colorado River near \nYuma. That event in 2023 involved snow melt. So, there was a more \nprolonged runoff, but this event has a larger volume of water, in \na much shorter time frame. Water levels and flow of this magnitude\nwill continue to impact several low water crossings and any \nsettlements, structures, and camps immediately near the banks. \nVery strong currents will also be very dangerous and deadly. There\nhave been multiple fatalities by people recreating in the Salt \nand Verde with past events. So, it is strongly encouraged to avoid\nentering the water and please obey any closures. Also, be \nprepared for significant flood water continuing through the Gila \nRiver to Painted Rock Dam in Maricopa County. From there it may \ntake a few days before water is released from Painted Rock into \nthe lower Gila in Yuma County.\n\nOutside of flooding along the rivers across south-centerl AZ over\nthe next several days, weather is expected to be calm and dry.\nAfternoon high temperatures across the lower deserts will continue\nto be in the upper 70s, to locally high, near 80, in SE CA through\nmost of the week. These temperatures are due to a low pressure \nsystem, currently just on the coastline of Northern CA, that is \nexpected to push inland and traverse across NV, and into the Four\nCorners area before exiting the region into the Plain states as \nearly as Thursday afternoon. While the low is not directly \nover the lower deserts, H5 heights will be suppressed with the \nlow passing to the north with height anomalies expected to be \nnear normal for early November by mid week. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \n\nThe long term forecast remains dry and seasonal. Ensemble guidance\ncontinues to show a dry positively-tilted trough dropping through\nthe area at the end of the week, ahead of a strong ridge spreading\ninto much of the west. Guidance also shows a cutoff low forming\nsouth of the ridge over the eastern Pacific, west of the Baja\nPeninsula. From here the models diverge a bit. A majority of the\nGEFS members bring the cutoff low up into the Desert Southwest,\ndrawing some moisture up with it. However, the rest of the global\nensemble guidance leans toward a much weaker cutoff low, with the\nDesert Southwest remaining dominated by ridging. Latest NBM PoPs\nthrough the long term forecast remain under 5% across the local\narea. Temperatures late-week through this weekend should warm a\nfew degrees, back to around normal as the ridge builds over the\nWest. Lower desert highs are forecast to be in the upper 70s to\nlower 80s. Breezy northerly winds are likely to develop heading\ninto the end of this week and weekend as surface high pressure\nbuilds over the Great Basin and Rockies, below the upper level\nridge, but wind magnitudes do not look to reach advisory level.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2320Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT; and\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected through the TAF\nperiod under FEW-SCT passing high clouds. Light and variable \nwinds are favored this evening, though winds may try to favor a \nlight W/NW component at times this evening before easterly winds \ntake hold by late evening. Across SE CA, winds at KIPL will favor \na W component, while directions favor the NW at KBLH, with \nextended periods of variability at both sites.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry seasonal conditions can be expected through the next 7 days,\nwith lingering flooding in major waterways like the Salt and Gila\nRivers. Saturated grounds will help keep surface humidity levels \nsomewhat elevated. Afternoon minimum RHs today will be around \n40-50% across AZ and 20-30% across southeast CA. Despite gradual \ndrying through the week, the minimum RHs will only fall to around \n30-40% across AZ and 17-25% across southeast CA by the end of the \nweek. Overnight recoveries will remain good through the week, with\naround 60-80% max RHs most nights. Winds through the next 7 days \nwill mostly follow typical diurnal trends with light speeds and \nmodest afternoon upslope gusts. Breezier north to northeast winds \nare anticipated late week into this weekend with gusts up to 20-30\nmph forecast at this time.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict/Ryan \nLONG TERM...Benedict \nAVIATION...Smith/Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict/Ryan\n",
